===Verb===
{{en-verb|head=[[move#Verb|move]] the [[goalpost]]s|moves the goalposts|moving the goalposts|moved the goalposts}}

# {{lb|en|idiomatic}} To [[alter]] the [[term#Noun|terms]] of an [[agreement]] or an [[agreed#Adjective|agreed]] [[target#Noun|target]], or the [[rule#Noun|rules]] of a [[negotiation]] while it is [[ongoing]], especially in an [[unfair]] way.
#: {{ux|en|This is a Government that meets its economic targets and does not '''move the goalposts'''.}}
#: {{ux|en|We were given a moving date, and an exchange date, but the buyers kept '''moving the goalposts''' and giving us a later date.}}
#* {{quote-journal|journal=Telephone Engineer and Management|location=Wheaton, Ill.|publisher=[[w:Harcourt (publisher)|Harcourt Brace Jovanovich Publications]]|year=1958|volume=62|page=64|oclc=2451041|passage=The tempo at which events are moving, the technological changes and the new developments being encountered on all sides sometimes gives the impression that the objectives or '''goal posts are constantly being moved'''.|brackets=on}}
#* {{quote-journal|magazine=[[w:Ladies' Home Journal|The Ladies’ Home Journal]]|location=Los Angeles, Calif.|publisher=Family Media, inc.|year=1977|volume=94|page=62|column=3|issn=0023-7124|oclc=890503999|passage=The conflict between being a lady and having a career was crystallized when I was in high school. It seemed as if my mother kept '''moving the goalposts''' around.}}
#* {{quote-book|author=W[illiam] C[lement] McGrew|chapter=What Chimpanzees Are, Are Not, and Might Be|title=Chimpanzee Material Culture: Implications for Human Evolution|location=Cambridge|publisher={{w|Cambridge University Press}}|year=1992|year_published=1996|page=217|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=gu7-DjJ5FbQC&pg=PA217|isbn=978-0-521-41303-9|passage=The common thread to these four problems is the question of wha can reasonably be inferred about the covert processes, as opposed to the overt acts, of other organisms if we and they cannot communicate directly through verbal disclosure. {{...}} No one baulks at applying the same standards of inference to other cultures of our species, but it is still easy to '''move the goalposts''' when another species is involved{{nb...}}.}}
#* {{quote-book|author=Peter G[osselin] Fitzgerald|authorlink=Peter Fitzgerald (politican)|chapter=Statement of Hon. Peter G. Fitzgerald, U.S. Senator from Illinois|title=Competition in the Telecommunications Industry: Hearing before the Committee on Commerce, Science, and Transportation, {{w|United States Senate}}, One Hundred Eighth Congress, First Session|location=Washington, D.C.|publisher=Printed for use of the [[w:United States Senate Committee on Commerce, Science, and Transportation|Committee on Commerce, Science, and Transportation]]; [[w:United States Government Publishing Office|U.S. Government Printing Office]]|date=14 January 2003|year_published=2006|page=85|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=cu-v1Rlf-PkC&pg=PA85|isbn=978-0-16-076170-6|passage=Sooner or later we have to have rules, and we have to know that they are not going to change, for people to have business plans developed and so that they can go out and raise capital and stick with their business model. But if there is this constant uncertainty that when the Commission changes, or when they have another rule tha they are going to '''move the goal posts''' again, nobody is going to want to have anything to do with this field.}}
#* {{quote-book|author=Mike Bradwell|title=The Reluctant Escapologist: Adventures in Alternative Theatre|location=London|publisher={{w|Nick Hern Books}}|year=2010|page=249|isbn=978-1-85459-538-6|passage=He had to put down a deposit of sixty grand to secure the theatre and just as he was about to sign the cheque, American Equity '''moved the goalposts''' yet again.}}
#* {{quote-book|author=Aaron Edlin|authorlink=Aaron Edlin|author2=Joseph Farrell|chapter=Freedom to Trade and the Competitive Process|editors=Roger D. Blair and D. Daniel Sokol|title=The Oxford Handbook of International Antitrust Economics|volume=1|location=Oxford|publisher={{w|Oxford University Press}}|year=2015|section=section 13.5 (Bundling and Loyalty Pricing)|pages=305–306|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=8W_DBAAAQBAJ&pg=PA305|isbn=978-0-19-985919-1|passage=When a practice harmfully '''moves the goalposts''', but does not violate freedom to trade, the ban on "unfair methods of competition" in Section 5 of the [[w:Federal Trade Commission Act of 1914|FTC Act]] might be a better fit than the ban on restraints of trade in the [[w:Sherman Antitrust Act|Sherman Act]].}}

